The organization SEKEM (Ancient Egyptian: 'vitality from the sun') was founded in 1977 by the Egyptian pharmacologist and social entrepreneur Dr. Ibrahim Abouleish in order to bring about cultural renewal in Egypt on a sustainable basis. Located northeast of Cairo, the organization now includes:
biodynamic farms;
trading companies for produce and processed foods (Hator and Libra), herbal teas and beauty products (ISIS Organic), medicinal herbs and medicines (ATOS Pharma), and organic cotton products (NatureTex);
a medical center;
a school based on the principles of Waldorf pedagogy open to pupils from any religious or ethnic background;
a community school catering specifically to the needs of children from disadvantaged groups;
a nursery
a vocational training center;
a college (Mahad Adult Education Training Institute) and research center (SEKEM Academy for Applied Art and Sciences);
Heliopolis University for Sustainable Development SEKEM's goals are to "restore and maintain the vitality of the soil and food as well as the biodiversity of nature" through sustainable, organic agriculture and to support social and cultural development in Egypt. Revenue from the trading companies grew from 37 million Egyptian pounds in 2000 to 100 million in 2003. By 2005, the organization had established a network of more than 2,000 farmers and numerous partner organizations in Egypt and began increasingly to seek to extend its "experience and acquired knowledge" to other countries, including India, Palestine, Senegal, Turkey, and - in partnership with the Fountain Foundation - South Africa.

McNair is a surname. Notable people with the surname include: 

- Scottish footballer and manager Alex McNair (born 1978), who played as a midfielder for Rangers, Manchester United and Blackburn Rovers in the Premier League, and for the Ghana national team;

- American football player Pat McNair (born 1966), who was a linebacker for the Baltimore Ravens and Kansas City Chiefs in the National Football League;

- British sailor and Olympic gold medalist Helen McNair (born 1948), who competed in the America's Cup and was a member of the British sailing team that won the Silver Medal in the 1972 Summer Olympics.

Weekday Religious Education is a released-time Christian education program for children aged 3-12 years old, offered on weekdays from 9:00am to 12:00pm. The program is delivered by a team of volunteers, and is funded by the Ministry of Education.

Weekday Religious Education (WER) has been offered in Ontario schools since 1983. WER provides an opportunity for children to explore their faith in a fun and stimulating environment, and to develop a lifelong spiritual foundation.

The objectives of WER are to:

(a) provide an opportunity for children to explore their faith; 
(b) promote understanding and respect for other religions; 
(c) develop a lifelong spiritual foundation; 
(d) provide children with the skills they need to participate actively in religious life; 
(e) provide parents with information about their children's religious education.

Sir Walter Egerton,  (1858 – 22 March 1947) had a long career in the administration of the British Empire, holding senior positions which included the Governorships of Lagos Colony (1904–1906), Southern Nigeria (1906–1912), and British Guiana (1912–1917). Early career
Egerton was born the only son of Walter Egerton, a gentleman of Exeter, Devon. After an education at Tonbridge School, in 1880 Egerton went out from England to the Straits Settlements as a cadet and for several years served there and in the protected states of Malaya. In 1880, aged about 21, he was a Magistrate at Singapore; in 1883 he became Collector at Penang; he was next a Commissioner of the Court of Requests at Penang and was appointed Acting Resident there in 1894;
In 1888, he was an acting First Magistrate at Penang, to which position he was later appointed. Egerton was admitted to the Middle Temple on 27 April 1888 and was Called to the Bar on 17 June 1896. He acted as the Colonial Secretary for the Straits Settlements between 1899 and 1901.

The Guede  also spelled Guédé or Guede is an Italian and French surname. Notable people with the surname include: 

1) Stefano Guede, an Italian murder suspect in the 2007 murder of British student Meredith Kercher; 
2) Marc Guede, a French co-defendant in the Kercher murder case; 
3) Raffaele Sollecito, an Italian co-defendant in the Kercher murder case; 
4) Rudy Guede, an American murderer who was convicted for the 1996 murder of 8-year-old Kimberly Leach in Tampa, Florida; 
5) Amanda Knox, an American woman who was acquitted of the 2007 murder of her British roommate Meredith Kercher.
